delta:
1e14.5 Msun. This lowers their baryon fractions and flattens their inner profiles. As a result P(k) is suppressed at k ≈ 0.1–10 h/Mpc relative to dark-matter-only runs. At higher k, cooling and star formation push the ratio back up.

**Link to halo baryon fractions.** Several groups find that the suppression is set mainly by the baryon fraction of group-scale haloes:

- the van Daalen et al. library of 92 simulation power spectra (arXiv:1906.00968);
- a halo-model treatment of observed gas profiles (arXiv:1908.05765);
- a BAHAMAS/cosmo-OWLS breakdown by halo mass (arXiv:2309.06479);
- an IllustrisTNG study (arXiv:2511.10634);
- the FLAMINGO resummation model (arXiv:2509.04552).

This ties the suppression to X-ray, tSZ and kSZ observables.

**Feedback variants and redshift.** FLAMINGO (arXiv:2410.17109) studies the baryon response across feedback variants calibrated to cluster gas fractions. It reports that the response grows as z decreases.

**Emulators and models.** Public options include:

- BACCO (arXiv:2011.15018);
- BCemu (arXiv:2108.08863);
- HMcode-2020, parametrised by log10 T_AGN (arXiv:2009.01858);
- an analytic redshift-independent model (arXiv:2504.15633).

CAMELS (arXiv:2010.00619) provides thousands of simulations with varied feedback.

**Weak lensing.** Ignoring these effects biases cosmic-shear cosmology (arXiv:1105.1075). The effect is also degenerate with neutrino mass (arXiv:1407.4301). Amon and Efstathiou (arXiv:2206.11794) and Preston, Amon and Efstathiou (arXiv:2305.09827) propose that stronger-than-standard suppression of non-linear power could reconcile KiDS and DES with Planck. FLAMINGO X-ray–shear work (arXiv:2602.02484) cites kSZ and eROSITA evidence for stronger feedback.

**Mitigation.** Approaches include:

- a theoretical-error covariance (arXiv:2410.12500);
- external constraints from SZ (arXiv:2301.02186);
- kSZ forecasts (arXiv:2401.03510);
- fast radio bursts (arXiv:2501.17922).

